## Read this first

The single most important fact about this design: **the source text of §4.2 was not readable from
this session.** `constitution/README.md:6-10` states the corpus "currently lives across
`derivation/`, `spec/`, and `reviews/`" and that consolidation into `constitution/PAEOS-0..6.md` is
a later (B0.3) task. My read scope was `constitution/`, `spec/`, `design/`; only `constitution/`
exists, and it contains a skeleton README plus two canaries.

Consequently the field set in [`envelope-fields.md`](envelope-fields.md) is a **reconstruction**,
derived from the three invariant violations named in the goal statement (authored confidence,
frequency-based promotion, deleted node) plus the constitution's own vocabulary. It is designed to
be *reconciled*, not trusted. [`assumptions.md`](assumptions.md) lists each assumed field with the
evidence that would confirm or refute it, and §7 of `design.md` defines the reconciliation gate that
IMPLEMENT must pass before the schema is declared frozen.

If §4.2 becomes readable before IMPLEMENT starts, run the reconciliation checklist
([`assumptions.md`](assumptions.md) §4) first. The component graph, layering, state machine
topology, and fixture-corpus architecture survive field renames; only the names change.
